1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,067
A volunteer spends an hour mentoring a

2
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:02,844
child.

3
00:00:03,541 --> 00:00:05,735
Before she leaves, she learns the family

4
00:00:05,793 --> 00:00:08,603
needs diapers tonight, so she posts a
brief

5
00:00:08,673 --> 00:00:09,311
update.

6
00:00:10,124 --> 00:00:12,411
By morning it is buried under dozens of

7
00:00:12,469 --> 00:00:13,282
messages.

8
00:00:14,234 --> 00:00:16,115
The hour may never be counted.

9
00:00:16,625 --> 00:00:18,669
The family may still be waiting.

10
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,773
This is the problem behind challenge five:

11
00:00:22,156 --> 00:00:24,792
coordinators rebuild their week from
chats,

12
00:00:24,908 --> 00:00:28,356
texts and photos — forty volunteers, four

13
00:00:28,402 --> 00:00:30,318
hours a week, by hand.

14
00:00:31,131 --> 00:00:33,754
Volunteer Relay turns those updates into

15
00:00:33,812 --> 00:00:36,819
traceable records, and needs someone owns.

16
00:00:37,771 --> 00:00:40,291
Here is the coordinator's inbox, with
review

17
00:00:40,337 --> 00:00:41,313
work waiting.

18
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:44,301
Volunteers report in their own words.

19
00:00:44,998 --> 00:00:47,099
This update describes one shared garden

20
00:00:47,134 --> 00:00:50,014
visit, two volunteers, and the time each
of

21
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:50,861
them gave.

22
00:00:51,674 --> 00:00:53,845
Relay proposes an activity and two

23
00:00:53,914 --> 00:00:56,643
contributions, and keeps every proposed

24
00:00:56,689 --> 00:00:58,721
field tied to the original message.

25
00:00:59,673 --> 00:01:02,552
Nothing is approved until staff decide.

26
00:01:03,933 --> 00:01:06,383
One message holds a visit for two people.

27
00:01:07,195 --> 00:01:10,423
One shared hour means one visit and two

28
00:01:10,516 --> 00:01:13,801
volunteer-hours, so approving it records
two

29
00:01:13,883 --> 00:01:16,065
separate contributions of sixty minutes

30
00:01:16,135 --> 00:01:16,634
each.

31
00:01:17,447 --> 00:01:20,582
Staff can inspect the evidence first, and

32
00:01:20,675 --> 00:01:24,076
anything missing or unresolved stays
visible

33
00:01:24,285 --> 00:01:25,562
instead of being guessed.

34
00:01:25,841 --> 00:01:27,060
Nothing is guessed.

35
00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:30,105
A later update describes the same visit.

36
00:01:31,057 --> 00:01:33,309
Counting every message would inflate the

37
00:01:33,356 --> 00:01:36,200
totals, so Relay raises the possible

38
00:01:36,247 --> 00:01:38,174
duplicate for staff review.

39
00:01:39,126 --> 00:01:41,320
Rejecting it leaves the approved ledger

40
00:01:41,436 --> 00:01:44,374
unchanged, and the decision stays attached

41
00:01:44,455 --> 00:01:45,546
to its source.

42
00:01:47,667 --> 00:01:50,082
An urgent need should never wait on a
buried

43
00:01:50,128 --> 00:01:50,837
message.

44
00:01:51,789 --> 00:01:54,436
Staff assign an owner, set an
acknowledgment

45
00:01:54,482 --> 00:01:56,967
deadline, and later record the outcome.

46
00:01:57,919 --> 00:02:00,229
If acknowledgment stalls, the worker

47
00:02:00,322 --> 00:02:01,854
escalates at the deadline.

48
00:02:02,806 --> 00:02:05,453
Delivery is not acknowledgment, and the

49
00:02:05,511 --> 00:02:07,578
board shows what still needs attention.

50
00:02:08,391 --> 00:02:11,107
Acknowledgment is a person, not a delivery

51
00:02:11,177 --> 00:02:11,955
receipt.

52
00:02:12,567 --> 00:02:15,202
At the end of the week the report comes
from

53
00:02:15,249 --> 00:02:17,757
the same records the team used to do its

54
00:02:17,803 --> 00:02:18,279
work.

55
00:02:19,231 --> 00:02:22,586
Approved hours, contributions, open needs

56
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:25,547
and pending items are counted
consistently,

57
00:02:26,058 --> 00:02:29,192
and unknown time stays unknown rather than

58
00:02:29,238 --> 00:02:30,376
becoming zero.

59
00:02:31,189 --> 00:02:33,627
Every decision is kept in an audit history

60
00:02:33,673 --> 00:02:36,251
with its source, so report and work share

61
00:02:36,321 --> 00:02:37,226
one source.

62
00:02:37,833 --> 00:02:39,098
Volunteer Relay.

63
00:02:39,795 --> 00:02:43,243
Every hour counted, every need connected.

64
00:02:44,056 --> 00:02:46,726
Built for nonprofit teams, with fictional

65
00:02:46,842 --> 00:02:50,047
demo data and an open-source
implementation.

66
00:02:50,859 --> 00:02:54,284
The repository holds the code, the plan
and

67
00:02:54,319 --> 00:02:55,573
the recording notes.
